{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:rstar:scout","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:437331531388514a19cee82bb0c92ccacd9d5b00f8ff841a7b1f171fe717d525","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.05},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.95},{"probability":0.9,"value":3}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"STARTING LEVELS, HARD DATA (FRED, daily, read 4 Nov 2026). DFII10 (10y TIPS real): 2.60% on 30 Oct 2026 — this verifies the locked 2A figure exactly — 2.62% on 2 Nov; the 13-day path 13 Oct to 2 Nov ran 2.88, 2.84, 2.78, 2.74, 2.80, 2.77, 2.76, 2.64, 2.66, 2.66, 2.64, 2.67, 2.63, 2.60, 2.62, i.e. a ~26bp rally INTO the print date, so 2.60% is the low of its own fortnight, not a representative level. DFII30: 3.08% on 30 Oct, 3.06% on 2 Nov. DGS10 nominal: 4.94% on 30 Oct, 4.91% on 2 Nov. T10YIE breakeven: 2.34% on 30 Oct, 2.29-2.30% on 2-3 Nov. The real 10s30s slope is therefore about +45bp and POSITIVE. GLOBAL LONG END: Japan 30y JGB hit an all-time high 4.235% intraday on 5 Oct 2026 (close 3.895% on 27 Oct, ~3.90% by 29 Oct), 10y JGB peaked near 3.12% on 1 and 6 Oct and traded near 2.84% on 29 Oct; one undated source reports UK 30y gilts \"briefly touched 5.75% for the first time since 1998\" — UNVERIFIED AT MY HORIZON for an October 2026 date, flagged as such. ACM term premium: NO October 2026 value found; nearest verifiable are 0.73% at 30 Apr 2026 and ~0.83% at 15 May 2026, CEIC showing 0.782 for January 2026; a 0.96% figure dated 18 Sep 2026 is attributed to KIM-WRIGHT, not ACM, and a 0.03% June 2026 figure names no model and I do not rely on it; a 1.76% year-end 2026 figure from Convextrade is \"a statistical projection, not an observed estimate\". So the ACM level is unverified at my horizon and I decompose without leaning on it. MANDATED DECOMPOSITION of the 2032-36 average, G7 GDP-weighted. COMPOSITION FIRST: this question is NOT the US question. Using approximate G7 GDP weights (US ~0.50, Japan ~0.11, Germany ~0.10, UK ~0.07, France ~0.07, Italy ~0.06, Canada ~0.05) and today's levels — US 2.60% (hard), Japan ~1.1% inferred from 10y nominal ~2.84% less a ~1.7% breakeven (INFERRED, unverified), euro core and Canada ~1.2-2.0% (UNVERIFIED) — the current G7 GDP-weighted 10y real yield is approximately 2.0%, roughly 60bp BELOW the US alone. Anchoring on DFII10 alone biases this question high by about that much, and I expect that to be the modal ensemble error. (1) r* component, G7 GDP-weighted short-run neutral real rate, 2032-36: P50 +0.90% (10-90 band roughly 0.30-1.60). Japan near 0, euro core 0.3-0.7, US 1.3-1.7. Life-cycle DISSAVING as the boomer cohort draws down pushes this up; precautionary saving under labour-income uncertainty, cognitive-labour compression depressing household income, and the Chinese and OECD saving gluts push it down; per 2A I explicitly allow a FRICTIONLESS r* that EASES while (r-g) still worsens. (2) Real term premium: P50 +1.10% (band 0.3-2.0). This is where the observable evidence is strongest and it is NOT an r* story: the positive real 10s30s slope, the synchronised multi-decade highs at the global long end (JGB 30y record, gilt 30y at 1998 highs), the 2A rollover-volume and convenience-yield-erosion channel, and levered hedge-fund basis-trade absorption as a fragile marginal buyer. The synchronisation across three independent sovereigns is the key inference: it rules out a US-idiosyncratic fiscal explanation and points to a common duration-supply/absorption factor, which is exactly a term premium. (3) Repression wedge: I treat financial repression as a genuine policy option with a probability, not a tail curiosity. P(at least one G7 sovereign applies MATERIAL duration targeting — an explicit yield cap, a purchase programme with a stated yield or spread objective, or a statutory sovereign-holding requirement on banks or pensions — at some point in 2027-2036) ~ 45%, given 2A's 3-year P95 Debt-at-Risk of ~117% of GDP, +2.4pp more under a 20% AI-led equity valuation shock. BUT the GDP-weighted wedge is small because the US is half the weight and is the least likely to adopt an explicit cap; Japan and the euro area have the instruments and the precedent. Expected wedge: -0.25pp (band 0 to -1.00pp). SUM: 0.90 + 1.10 - 0.25 = 1.75%. I then shade UP to 1.95% because the lens weights the observed trend: G7 real yields have risen monotonically in regime terms since 2021 and are making multi-decade long-end highs with policy TIGHTENING (Fed hiked 16 Sep 2026, held 10-2 on 28 Oct with both dissents for a further hike, core PCE 3.1%), and \"mean reversion to a low r*\" is a model claim, not an observation. VARIANCE ATTRIBUTION as required: real term premium sigma ~55bp and repression wedge sigma ~35bp (bimodal, not Gaussian) together carry roughly 80% of my variance; r* sigma ~30bp carries the rest. The variance in this question is a POLICY-REGIME variable, not a savings-and-investment variable. P10 1.05 (coupled capex bust per AI-Q4 plus BIS-style demand shortfall plus a restarted purchase programme) / P50 1.95 / P90 3.00 (fiscal dominance with a term-premium spike and no repression response).","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:57.965Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"FRED DFII10 (10y TIPS real yield, daily, hard market data) records a monthly average below 1.60% for six consecutive months — against 2.60% observed on 30 Oct 2026.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Directly falsifies my core claim that real yields do not mean-revert downward. CQ4 P50 down from 1.95% to about 1.30% and P90 down ~60bp; CQ6-D up by roughly +8 and CQ6-C down by a similar amount; CQ3 P50 down ~0.2pp via the weaker-demand channel that would have produced the rally.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The US REAL curve flattens to inversion: DFII30 minus DFII10 at or below 0bp on a 3-month average, against roughly +45bp observed (3.07% vs 2.62% on 2 Nov 2026).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my variance attribution specifically — it would say the level is being driven by expected short rates (r*) rather than by duration absorption. Shifts ~30 points of my CQ4 variance budget from the term-premium/repression pair back to r*, cuts CQ4 P90 by ~50bp while leaving P50 roughly unchanged, and weakens CQ6-B's \\\"steep real term premia\\\" clause in favour of C or D.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An explicit material duration-targeting action by any G7 sovereign or central bank: a published yield cap or band, a reinstated purchase programme with a STATED yield or spread objective rather than a quantity objective, or a statutory sovereign-holding requirement imposed on banks, insurers or pension funds.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Confirms the repression branch I priced at ~45% but falsifies my choice to keep the GDP-weighted wedge small at -0.25pp. CQ4 P50 down ~40bp and P90 down ~70bp (the upper tail is what repression removes); CQ6-C up ~+8 (financial repression is named in C); CQ5 conjunct 1 down, since a capped long end suppresses realised MOVE.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"GDP-weighted OECD-38 core inflation (constructed from BEA core PCE, Eurostat core HICP, and national core series) prints at or below 2.0% y/y for four consecutive quarters — against core PCE 3.1% and core CPI 2.6% observed for September 2026.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the front-loading argument that carries my CQ2 conjunct-1 marginal of 58%. CQ2 down ~12 points to roughly 17; CQ6-D up ~+7 and CQ6-C down ~+-7; weakens plank (c)'s first clause while vindicating its second, so my hypothesis score for (c) would rise rather than fall.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE monthly average at or above 120 in at least 12 of the 24 months to Dec 31 2028 — against roughly 70.9 on 17 Jul 2026 and a 2026 range of about 65-103 (VENDOR/INDEX series, level unverified at my horizon).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my binding CQ5 constraint, which is conjunct 1 rather than the drawdown conjunct. CQ5 up ~+12 to roughly 30; also raises my CQ4 P90 by ~30bp, since a sustained high-vol rates regime is the observable footprint of the term-premium blowout branch.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI (NBS monthly, hard administrative) prints at or above 2.0% y/y for six consecutive months, following the observed September 2026 reading of +0.9% with PPI at +5.0% y/y.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies CQ2's second conjunct outright for much of the window. CQ2 down ~8 points; raises my assessment of Chinese nominal demand and hence CQ3's China leg by ~0.2pp at the P50, pushing a little mass from CQ6-D toward B.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 1 scout seat, effort 'rstar'."}}